## generating video script
2026-01-16 23:45:08 | DEBUG | "./app\services\task.py:35": generate_script - video script: 
Blood sugar control is truly crucial for type 2 diabetes. Diabetes medications can be broadly categorized into those that promote insulin secretion, improve insulin resistance, or inhibit glucose absorption. Oral medications alone come in a wide variety. Metformin is the most basic and commonly used, reducing glucose production in the liver and increasing tissue insulin sensitivity. Sulfonylureas stimulate the pancreas to release more insulin. DPP-4 inhibitors prevent the breakdown of the naturally occurring GLP-1 hormone in our bodies, thereby promoting insulin secretion and suppressing glucagon secretion to lower blood sugar. Additionally, SGLT-2 inhibitors play a remarkable role in effectively lowering blood sugar by preventing the reabsorption of glucose in the kidneys, allowing it to be excreted in urine. GLP-1 receptor agonists act similarly to our body's own GLP-1, increasing insulin secretion and slowing gastric emptying to aid in the slower absorption of glucose. They even offer the potential for weight loss, bringing hope to many. Among injectable medications, insulin therapy is the most representative. Depending on their action time, various types of insulin exist, including ultra-rapid-acting, rapid-acting, intermediate-acting, and long-acting, which are meticulously adjusted to suit the patient's condition and lifestyle. They can be used relatively conveniently with devices like insulin pens, and are essential not only for type 1 diabetes patients but also for type 2 diabetes patients when oral medications are insufficient for blood sugar control. Diabetes treatment should be personalized based on the individual's condition and the progression of the disease. It is important to establish an optimal treatment plan that goes beyond simply lowering blood sugar levels, considering additional benefits such as preventing cardiovascular disease or managing weight. We hope you maintain a healthy life through consistent management and close communication with your doctor.
