The very first thing to exclude from a cancer patient's diet is processed meat. Processed meats like ham, sausage, and bacon generate carcinogenic nitrosamines during their manufacturing process, which can induce inflammation and promote the growth of cancer cells. Since they can place an unnecessary burden on an already weakened body and even increase the risk of cancer recurrence, they must be resolutely avoided. Next, sugar and refined carbohydrates are cancer cells' favorite food. Foods that rapidly raise blood sugar, such as cake, sugary drinks, and white bread, elevate insulin levels, stimulating cancer cell proliferation and worsening inflammatory responses. To win the fight against cancer, a war against sugar is essential, and minimizing the intake of unhealthy sugars is extremely important. The third category is fried foods and trans fats. French fries or chicken fried at high temperatures not only produce carcinogens like acrylamide but also increase reactive oxygen species that cause cell damage. Especially trans fats, found in margarine and shortening, can maximize inflammation and disrupt the immune system, becoming a major culprit that hinders cancer treatment. Lastly, any form of alcohol can be detrimental, even deadly, for cancer patients. Alcohol places a heavy burden on the liver, impairing its detoxification function and weakening immunity, thereby creating an environment conducive to cancer cell growth. It not only increases the risk of cancer development and recurrence but can also exacerbate side effects during treatment, so even a single sip should be avoided.
